var TEACH = (typeof TEACH === "undefined") ? {} : TEACH;

TEACH["8.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"There have never been this many old people. Not in Britain, not anywhere, not at any point in human history. Every problem in this specialty follows from that one fact.",
  teach:[
    "Around a fifth of the UK population is aged 65 or over, and the proportion continues to rise. The over-85 group is the fastest growing segment in both absolute and proportional terms.",
    "The oldest old, those over 85, matter disproportionately because dementia prevalence roughly doubles every five years after 65, so the fastest-growing group carries the highest risk.",
    "Dementia prevalence rises from around 1 to 2 per cent at 65 to around 20 to 25 per cent or more above 85.",
    "Population ageing has two causes: falling fertility and rising life expectancy. Falling fertility contributes more than longevity to the changing age structure.",
    "The old age dependency ratio, the number of people of pensionable age per hundred of working age, is the standard measure of the economic consequence, and it is rising across all high-income countries.",
    "Compression of morbidity, Fries' hypothesis, proposes that the onset of disability is postponed faster than death, so people live longer with fewer years of disability. The evidence for this is mixed rather than settled.",
    "The competing expansion of morbidity account holds that medical advance keeps people alive with disease, extending years lived with disability. The truth appears to differ by condition.",
    "Globally the shift is faster in low- and middle-income countries than it was in Europe, giving those countries far less time to build services. Around two-thirds of people with dementia worldwide live in low- and middle-income countries.",
    "Age-specific dementia incidence appears to have fallen in several high-income countries over recent decades, probably through better cardiovascular health and education, while absolute numbers continue to rise because there are more old people.",
    "That distinction between falling age-specific incidence and rising absolute numbers is examinable and frequently confused.",
    "Life expectancy gains in the UK stalled from around 2011, and healthy life expectancy has not risen with total life expectancy, so the added years are frequently years of ill health.",
    "Socioeconomic inequality in healthy life expectancy is very large, with differences of around two decades between the most and least deprived areas in England."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How does dementia prevalence change with age?","It roughly doubles every five years after 65, rising from around 1 to 2 per cent at 65 to 20 to 25 per cent or more above 85. The over-85 group is the fastest growing segment of the population, which is why absolute numbers rise steeply."],
    ["Distinguish falling incidence from rising prevalence of dementia.","Age-specific dementia incidence appears to have fallen in several high-income countries, probably through improved cardiovascular health and education, while the absolute number of people with dementia continues to rise because the population contains far more older people."],
    ["Contrast compression and expansion of morbidity.","Fries' compression of morbidity hypothesis holds that the onset of disability is postponed faster than death is, so people live longer with proportionally fewer disabled years. The expansion of morbidity account holds that medical advance keeps people alive with disease, increasing years lived with disability. The evidence differs by condition."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Around a fifth of the UK population is aged 65 or over.",
    "Falling fertility contributes more than rising longevity to population ageing.",
    "Around two-thirds of people with dementia worldwide live in low- and middle-income countries.",
    "UK life expectancy gains stalled from around 2011, and healthy life expectancy has not kept pace.",
    "Healthy life expectancy differs by around two decades between the most and least deprived areas of England."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","companion"] };

TEACH["8.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Old age psychiatry exists because an eighty-year-old with depression is a different clinical problem from a thirty-year-old with depression: different drugs, different physical illnesses, different social situation, and a family who needs help as much as the patient does.",
  teach:[
    "The specialty was recognised in the UK in 1989, and the model developed by Tom Arie at Goodmayes and later Nottingham is the origin of the community-based multidisciplinary approach.",
    "The founding principles: home assessment as the default, a multidisciplinary team, close working with geriatric medicine and social services, and support for carers as part of the work rather than an addition to it.",
    "The case for a separate specialty: distinct epidemiology with dementia and delirium dominant; distinct pharmacology; multimorbidity and polypharmacy; different legal issues centred on capacity; and the central role of carers.",
    "The case against: ageism in the form of a service defined by age rather than by need; disruption at the transition point; and the risk that general adult services stop developing skills in older people.",
    "Several UK services have moved to needs-based rather than age-based access, and the College has debated this at length without a settled national position.",
    "Community mental health teams for older people are the backbone, with memory services, liaison services in the general hospital, inpatient wards separated into functional and organic, and intensive home treatment.",
    "Memory assessment services are the recommended point of diagnosis under NICE, providing assessment, diagnosis, and post-diagnostic support rather than diagnosis alone.",
    "Liaison psychiatry for older people in the general hospital is high-value work, since a large proportion of older inpatients have delirium, dementia or depression, and the RAID model in Birmingham demonstrated substantial cost savings through reduced length of stay.",
    "Working with geriatric medicine is not optional. The overlap is greatest in delirium, falls, frailty and end-of-life care, and joint working produces better outcomes than referral between services.",
    "Frailty as a concept has become central: a state of reduced physiological reserve in which a minor stressor produces disproportionate deterioration. The Clinical Frailty Scale is the common language.",
    "Social services provide domiciliary care, day services, respite and residential and nursing home placement. Continuing healthcare funding decisions are a recurrent source of dispute and delay.",
    "Voluntary sector organisations, particularly the Alzheimer's Society and Age UK, provide advice, befriending, day activity and carer support that statutory services do not.",
    "Carers: the majority of care for people with dementia is provided unpaid by family. Carer strain predicts institutionalisation more strongly than the severity of the patient's illness does.",
    "The START intervention, a manualised coping intervention for family carers of people with dementia, reduced carer depression and anxiety with effects sustained at long-term follow-up, and is cost-effective.",
    "Carers have a statutory right to a carer's assessment in their own right under the Care Act 2014, irrespective of the eligibility of the person cared for."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What predicts institutionalisation in dementia more strongly than illness severity?","Carer strain. The level of distress and burden experienced by the family carer predicts admission to residential care more strongly than the severity of the person's dementia."],
    ["What is the START intervention?","A manualised coping intervention for family carers of people with dementia, which reduced carer depression and anxiety with effects sustained at long-term follow-up and demonstrated cost-effectiveness."],
    ["Give the arguments for and against a separate old age psychiatry service.","For: distinct epidemiology dominated by dementia and delirium, distinct pharmacology, multimorbidity and polypharmacy, capacity-centred legal issues, and the central role of carers. Against: age-based rather than needs-based access is a form of ageism, transition at an arbitrary age disrupts care, and general adult services may lose skills in older people."],
    ["What was demonstrated by the RAID liaison model?","That a comprehensive older adult liaison psychiatry service in a general hospital produced substantial cost savings through reduced length of stay and readmission, given the high prevalence of delirium, dementia and depression among older inpatients."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Old age psychiatry was recognised as a UK specialty in 1989; Tom Arie developed the community model.",
    "Memory assessment services are the NICE-recommended point of diagnosis.",
    "Frailty is reduced physiological reserve; the Clinical Frailty Scale is the common measure.",
    "Most dementia care is provided unpaid by family members.",
    "Carers have a statutory right to assessment under the Care Act 2014."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","nice","companion"] };

TEACH["8.3"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Assessing an older person takes longer and involves more people. You need someone who knew them before, you need the drug chart, and you need to see them where they live, because the house tells you things the interview will not.",
  teach:[
    "Home assessment is the default in this specialty, not a special measure. The home reveals self-care, nutrition, safety, hoarding, alcohol, medication adherence and the reality of the support available.",
    "Collateral history is essential rather than desirable. What the person was like six months ago is the single most informative piece of data, and only someone who knew them can supply it.",
    "Establish the timeline explicitly: gradual decline over years suggests neurodegeneration, stepwise decline suggests vascular disease, and onset over days suggests delirium.",
    "Sensory impairment must be corrected before assessment. Hearing aids in, glasses on, adequate lighting. Undetected deafness is repeatedly mistaken for cognitive impairment and for paranoia.",
    "Hearing loss is an independent modifiable risk factor for dementia, and hearing aid use is associated with reduced risk in observational studies.",
    "Medication review is part of the psychiatric assessment. Anticholinergic burden is a specific and measurable risk, and anticholinergic drugs impair cognition and increase dementia risk.",
    "Common culprits with anticholinergic effects: oxybutynin, amitriptyline, older antihistamines, some antipsychotics, and procyclidine. The Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden scale quantifies the load.",
    "Physical examination is not optional: cardiovascular status, neurological examination including gait, signs of parkinsonism, and evidence of falls, dehydration, infection or pain.",
    "Gait is highly informative. A shuffling gait suggests parkinsonism, a magnetic gait suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus, and gait slowing accompanies vascular cognitive impairment.",
    "Cognitive testing: MMSE is widely known but copyright-restricted; MoCA is more sensitive to mild impairment and to executive dysfunction; ACE-III gives domain scores; and the 6-CIT and GPCOG are brief primary care tools.",
    "Test scores must be interpreted against education, first language, sensory impairment, and premorbid ability. A highly educated person with a normal MMSE may have substantial decline from their own baseline.",
    "Informant-rated instruments such as the IQCODE and, in Down syndrome, the DLD and DSQIID, are essential where formal testing is unusable.",
    "Depression screening: the Geriatric Depression Scale, which excludes somatic items, and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present.",
    "Investigations in cognitive impairment: full blood count, urea and electrolytes, calcium, glucose or HbA1c, liver and thyroid function, B12 and folate, with syphilis and HIV serology where indicated, and structural imaging.",
    "Functional assessment matters more than test scores for decisions. Activities of daily living, instrumental activities such as finances, cooking, medication and driving, and observed rather than reported performance.",
    "Occupational therapy home assessment provides functional information that no clinic assessment can, and often changes the plan more than any investigation.",
    "Capacity assessment is routine here: capacity for treatment, for accommodation decisions, for finances, and for driving, each assessed separately and at the relevant time."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Why is home assessment the default in old age psychiatry?","Because the home reveals self-care, nutrition, hygiene, safety, hoarding, alcohol use, medication adherence and the reality of available support, none of which is reliably obtained in a clinic. It also provides the functional information that determines decisions."],
    ["Which cognitive instruments are used, and how do they differ?","MMSE is widely known but copyright-restricted; MoCA is more sensitive to mild impairment and executive dysfunction; ACE-III gives domain-level scores; and 6-CIT and GPCOG are brief primary care screens. All must be interpreted against education, first language, sensory impairment and premorbid ability."],
    ["What is anticholinergic burden and why does it matter?","The cumulative anticholinergic effect of a person's total medication, quantified by scales such as the Anticholinergic Cognitive Burden scale. Higher burden impairs cognition, increases falls and delirium, and is associated with increased dementia risk. Common contributors include oxybutynin, amitriptyline, older antihistamines and procyclidine."],
    ["Which depression instruments are appropriate in older people?","The Geriatric Depression Scale, which excludes somatic items confounded by physical illness, and the Cornell Scale for Depression in Dementia, which is informant-based and is the correct instrument where dementia is present."],
    ["What does gait examination contribute?","A shuffling festinant gait suggests parkinsonism; a magnetic or apraxic gait suggests normal pressure hydrocephalus; and gait slowing accompanies vascular cognitive impairment. Gait is among the most informative single elements of the physical examination."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Correct sensory impairment before assessing cognition; deafness is mistaken for impairment and for paranoia.",
    "Hearing loss is an independent modifiable risk factor for dementia.",
    "IQCODE is the informant-rated instrument where formal testing is unusable.",
    "A stepwise timeline suggests vascular disease; days suggests delirium; years suggests neurodegeneration.",
    "Occupational therapy home assessment often changes the plan more than any investigation."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","nice","lishman"] };

TEACH["8.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"In old age the body and the mind stop being separable problems. A stroke changes personality, Parkinson's causes hallucinations, deafness causes paranoia, and a thyroid problem looks exactly like dementia.",
  teach:[
    "Parkinson's disease: depression affects around 40 per cent and frequently precedes motor symptoms by years, being part of the disease rather than a reaction to it.",
    "Apathy in Parkinson's disease is distinct from depression and is often mistaken for it. Apathy is loss of motivation without sadness or negative cognition, and it responds poorly to antidepressants.",
    "Psychosis in Parkinson's disease: visual hallucinations, often of people or animals, occur in a large proportion with longer disease duration, arising from both the disease and dopaminergic treatment.",
    "Impulse control disorders from dopamine agonists: pathological gambling, hypersexuality, compulsive shopping and binge eating, occurring in a substantial minority and reversible on dose reduction.",
    "Parkinson's disease dementia develops in the majority with long disease duration. The one-year rule distinguishes it from dementia with Lewy bodies: dementia beginning more than a year after motor onset is Parkinson's disease dementia.",
    "Cerebrovascular disease: post-stroke depression in around a third, associated with worse functional recovery and higher mortality independent of stroke severity.",
    "Emotionalism, pathological crying or laughing, occurs in around a fifth after stroke, is distinct from depression, and responds rapidly to low-dose antidepressants.",
    "Vascular cognitive impairment covers the spectrum from mild deficits to vascular dementia, and the risk factors are the modifiable cardiovascular ones, which makes this the most preventable dementia.",
    "Sensory impairment: hearing loss is associated with depression, social withdrawal, paranoid ideation and increased dementia risk. Visual impairment is associated with depression, falls and Charles Bonnet syndrome.",
    "Charles Bonnet syndrome: complex formed visual hallucinations in people with visual impairment, with intact cognition and full insight. It is not psychosis and requires explanation and reassurance rather than antipsychotics.",
    "Emotional reaction to physical illness: adjustment disorder, demoralisation, loss of role and independence, and anticipatory grief. Demoralisation is distinguishable from depression by preserved capacity for pleasure when circumstances permit.",
    "Loss of independence is often the pivotal event rather than the illness itself, and this is frequently missed when services focus on the diagnosis.",
    "Reversible and secondary dementias must always be considered. The classic screen covers B12 and folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, neurosyphilis, HIV, normal pressure hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma, and depression.",
    "Normal pressure hydrocephalus: the triad of gait apraxia, urinary incontinence and cognitive impairment, with gait usually first. It is treatable by shunting, and gait improvement is the best predictor of shunt response.",
    "Truly reversible dementias are uncommon, of the order of a few per cent, but the consequences of missing one are severe enough to justify the screen in every case.",
    "Depressive pseudodementia is the most important reversible cause: abrupt onset, don't know answers, complaint of memory rather than concealment, and improvement with antidepressant treatment.",
    "The relationship is not simple, though. A substantial proportion of people with depressive pseudodementia go on to develop dementia within several years, so the finding warrants follow-up rather than reassurance."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Distinguish apathy from depression in Parkinson's disease.","Apathy is loss of motivation, initiative and emotional responsiveness without sadness, guilt or negative cognitions. Depression includes low mood and negative cognition. Apathy responds poorly to antidepressants and is frequently misdiagnosed as depression."],
    ["State the one-year rule.","Dementia beginning more than one year after the onset of parkinsonian motor symptoms is classified as Parkinson's disease dementia. Dementia beginning before, at the same time as, or within a year of motor onset is classified as dementia with Lewy bodies."],
    ["What is Charles Bonnet syndrome and how is it managed?","Complex formed visual hallucinations occurring in people with visual impairment, with intact cognition and full insight. It is not a psychosis and is managed by explanation and reassurance, optimisation of vision and lighting, rather than by antipsychotic medication."],
    ["What causes should be screened for as potentially reversible dementia?","B12 and folate deficiency, hypothyroidism, hypercalcaemia, neurosyphilis, HIV, normal pressure hydrocephalus, subdural haematoma and depression. Truly reversible causes are uncommon, of the order of a few per cent, but the consequences of missing one justify screening in every case."],
    ["Give the triad of normal pressure hydrocephalus and the best predictor of shunt response.","Gait apraxia, urinary incontinence and cognitive impairment, with gait disturbance usually appearing first. Gait improvement following a large-volume lumbar puncture is the best predictor of a good response to shunting."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Depression affects around 40 per cent in Parkinson's disease and may precede motor symptoms.",
    "Dopamine agonists cause impulse control disorders reversible on dose reduction.",
    "Post-stroke depression affects around a third and worsens functional recovery independent of severity.",
    "Emotionalism responds rapidly to low-dose antidepressants.",
    "A substantial proportion of people with depressive pseudodementia later develop dementia, so follow-up is required."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","lishman","oxneuropsy"] };

TEACH["8.6"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Older men have the highest suicide rate of any group in most countries. They give fewer warnings, use more lethal methods, and are far less likely to have been asked.",
  teach:[
    "Suicide rates rise with age in men in most countries, and men over 75 have among the highest rates of any demographic group.",
    "In the UK the highest male rate has shifted towards middle age in recent decades, but the older male rate remains high and the international pattern of rising rate with age persists.",
    "The ratio of attempts to completed suicide falls sharply with age. In young people it is of the order of 200 to 1; in older people it may be closer to 4 to 1.",
    "That single figure changes clinical practice: an act of self-harm in an older person must be treated as a serious suicide attempt until demonstrated otherwise.",
    "Older people use more lethal methods, are more likely to be alone, take more precautions against discovery, and are physically less able to survive an attempt.",
    "Warning behaviours are less prominent. Older people communicate intent less often, and when they do it is more often to a general practitioner than to a mental health service.",
    "The great majority of older people who die by suicide have seen their general practitioner in the preceding months, and a substantial proportion within the preceding month. This is the principal missed opportunity.",
    "Depression is present in the great majority of older people who die by suicide, and is frequently untreated or undertreated rather than unrecognised.",
    "Risk factors: male sex, depression, physical illness particularly with pain or functional impairment, bereavement especially in the first year, social isolation, alcohol misuse, and access to means.",
    "Painful and disabling physical illness is a much stronger contributor than in younger groups, and malignancy, neurological disease and chronic pain are specifically associated.",
    "Hopelessness is a stronger predictor than depression severity, and it is what should be asked about directly.",
    "The distinction between wanting to die and wanting the situation to end is clinically useful and is frequently the opening for a real conversation.",
    "Assessment is the same in principle but the threshold for concern must be lower, and physical illness must never be allowed to render suicidal thinking understandable and therefore unaddressed.",
    "Treating depression in older people reduces suicidal ideation, and depression in this group responds to treatment as well as it does in younger people, contrary to a persistent clinical assumption.",
    "Means restriction applies here too: review of stockpiled medication, particularly hoarded analgesics and hypnotics, is a specific and effective intervention.",
    "Assisted dying legislation is a live policy question in the UK and elsewhere. The relationship between requests for hastened death, treatable depression, and inadequate palliative care is central to the debate and should be described neutrally."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How does the ratio of attempts to completed suicide change with age?","It falls sharply. In young people the ratio is of the order of 200 to 1, whereas in older people it may be closer to 4 to 1. Any act of self-harm in an older person must therefore be treated as a serious suicide attempt until demonstrated otherwise."],
    ["What is the principal missed opportunity in older adult suicide prevention?","Primary care contact. The great majority of older people who die by suicide have consulted their general practitioner in the preceding months, and a substantial proportion within the preceding month, most commonly with depression that is untreated or undertreated."],
    ["Which risk factors are specific to older adults?","Painful and disabling physical illness, particularly malignancy, neurological disease and chronic pain; bereavement especially within the first year; loss of independence; social isolation; and access to stockpiled medication. Male sex and depression carry weight as at any age."],
    ["Which cognitive feature predicts suicide best in older people?","Hopelessness, which predicts more strongly than depression severity and should be asked about directly. The distinction between wanting to die and wanting the current situation to end is clinically useful."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Men over 75 have among the highest suicide rates of any demographic group internationally.",
    "Older people give fewer warnings, use more lethal methods and take more precautions against discovery.",
    "Depression in older people responds to treatment as well as in younger people.",
    "Review of stockpiled medication is a specific and effective means restriction intervention.",
    "Physical illness must never render suicidal thinking understandable and therefore unaddressed."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","nice","companion"] };

TEACH["8.7"] = { free:false,
  simple:"People do not become different people when they get old. Traits soften a little and personality disorder becomes less dramatic, but the person who was difficult at forty is usually the person who is difficult at eighty, in a smaller arena.",
  teach:[
    "Personality traits are broadly stable across the lifespan, with modest normative change: neuroticism and extraversion decline slightly, agreeableness and conscientiousness rise slightly into later life.",
    "This pattern, the maturity principle, is replicated across cultures and suggests some genuine normative development rather than pure stability.",
    "Personality disorder does not disappear with age. Prevalence in older community samples is around 10 per cent, similar to younger adults, though the presentation changes.",
    "Cluster B presentations attenuate with age, particularly the impulsive and behavioural features, in the same burnout pattern seen in antisocial personality disorder.",
    "Cluster A and Cluster C presentations persist or become more prominent, since dependency, avoidance and suspiciousness are amplified rather than reduced by loss of independence.",
    "Diagnostic criteria are poorly suited to older people. Items referring to occupational functioning, sexual behaviour and impulsive risk taking are inapplicable, so criterion-based diagnosis under-detects.",
    "The behaviours through which personality disorder expresses itself change with the environment. In a care home the expression is refusal of care, complaint, splitting of staff and hoarding rather than the presentations seen at thirty.",
    "Personality change in later life is a red flag, not a variant of ageing. New apathy, disinhibition, coarsening of behaviour or loss of empathy suggests frontotemporal dementia, frontal lesion, stroke or another organic cause.",
    "Frontotemporal dementia commonly presents as personality change with preserved memory, and is frequently mislabelled as a late-life personality disorder or as a midlife crisis for years before diagnosis.",
    "Personality influences the presentation and course of every other disorder in this age group: adjustment to illness, engagement with rehabilitation, adherence, and tolerance of dependency all track personality.",
    "Personality also shapes carer relationships and predicts placement breakdown in residential care.",
    "Erikson's final stage, ego integrity versus despair, provides a developmental frame: the task of later life is to accept one's own life as having been meaningful, and despair follows failure of that task.",
    "Life review and reminiscence work draw directly on Erikson's account and have a genuine evidence base in late-life depression.",
    "Assessment requires longitudinal informant history. A personality disorder diagnosed for the first time at eighty on the basis of current behaviour alone is very likely to be something else."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How do personality traits change normatively with age?","Neuroticism and extraversion decline slightly while agreeableness and conscientiousness rise slightly into later life. This maturity principle is replicated across cultures and represents modest genuine development rather than pure stability."],
    ["How does personality disorder present differently in older people?","Cluster B presentations attenuate, particularly impulsive and behavioural features. Cluster A and Cluster C presentations persist or become more prominent, since dependency, avoidance and suspiciousness are amplified by loss of independence. Expression shifts to refusal of care, complaint, staff splitting and hoarding rather than the presentations seen in younger adults."],
    ["What does new personality change in later life indicate?","An organic cause until proven otherwise. New apathy, disinhibition, coarsening of behaviour or loss of empathy suggests frontotemporal dementia, frontal lesion, stroke or another neurological process. Frontotemporal dementia frequently presents as personality change with preserved memory."],
    ["What is Erikson's final developmental stage, and what follows from it clinically?","Ego integrity versus despair: the task of accepting one's own life as having been meaningful and coherent. Failure of that task produces despair. It underpins life review and reminiscence approaches, which have a genuine evidence base in late-life depression."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Personality disorder prevalence in older community samples is around 10 per cent.",
    "Criteria referring to occupation, sexual behaviour and impulsive risk taking under-detect in older people.",
    "Personality predicts adjustment to illness, adherence and placement breakdown.",
    "A first diagnosis of personality disorder at eighty on current behaviour alone is very likely to be something else.",
    "Longitudinal informant history is required for the diagnosis in this age group."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","tyrer11"] };

TEACH["8.8"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Older people were long thought to be unsuitable for therapy, on the grounds that they were too set in their ways and had too little future left. Both claims were wrong, and the outcomes are as good as in younger adults.",
  teach:[
    "Freud held that people over about fifty lacked the elasticity of mental process required for analysis. This claim was made without evidence and has been thoroughly refuted.",
    "Outcomes of psychological therapy in older adults are equivalent to those in younger adults across CBT, IPT and problem-solving therapy, in depression and in anxiety.",
    "Older people are systematically under-referred to psychological therapy services, which is the practical form the old prejudice now takes.",
    "Practical adaptations: slower pace, shorter sessions where fatigue or concentration require it, more repetition and written summaries, larger print materials, attention to hearing and vision, and accessible venues or home delivery.",
    "Cognitive adaptations: reduce reliance on written homework where literacy or vision limits it, use concrete examples, and involve a family member where memory limits between-session recall.",
    "Content differs. Common themes are loss, bereavement, physical illness and pain, loss of role and identity, dependency, fear of becoming a burden, fear of dying, and unresolved material from a long life.",
    "Cohort effects matter: an eighty-five-year-old grew up when psychological language did not exist as it does now, and may find the framing unfamiliar rather than unacceptable.",
    "Transference in work with older adults frequently reverses the usual pattern. The patient may relate to a younger therapist as a child or grandchild rather than as a parent figure.",
    "Countertransference is often the greater difficulty. Therapists face their own parents, their own ageing and their own mortality, and may respond with excessive protectiveness, therapeutic pessimism, or avoidance of the topic of death.",
    "Therapeutic nihilism is the commonest countertransference failure: the belief that at this age nothing can change, which is contradicted by the outcome evidence.",
    "Life review therapy, from Butler, is a structured process of reviewing and reappraising the life course, with a genuine evidence base in late-life depression.",
    "Reminiscence work differs from life review in being less structured and more social, and is widely used in dementia care with modest evidence for mood and quality of life.",
    "CBT is effective in late-life depression and anxiety, with adaptations. Problem-solving therapy has particularly good evidence in depression with executive dysfunction, where standard cognitive work is harder.",
    "Behavioural activation is effective, simple to deliver and well suited to older people, including those with mild cognitive impairment.",
    "Group therapy addresses isolation directly, which is a specific advantage in this population, since isolation is often a maintaining factor rather than only a consequence.",
    "In dementia, cognitive stimulation therapy has NICE support with evidence for cognition and quality of life, and is one of the few non-pharmacological interventions with a solid evidence base."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the evidence for psychological therapy in older adults?","Outcomes are equivalent to those in younger adults across CBT, interpersonal therapy and problem-solving therapy, in both depression and anxiety. Freud's claim that people over fifty lacked the mental elasticity for therapy was made without evidence and has been refuted. Older people remain systematically under-referred."],
    ["How does transference and countertransference differ in work with older adults?","Transference frequently reverses the usual pattern, with the patient relating to a younger therapist as a child or grandchild rather than as a parent figure. Countertransference is often the greater difficulty, as the therapist confronts their own parents, their own ageing and their own mortality, producing excessive protectiveness, avoidance of death as a topic, or therapeutic nihilism."],
    ["Which psychological therapy is best suited to depression with executive dysfunction?","Problem-solving therapy, which has particularly good evidence in late-life depression with executive impairment, where standard cognitive restructuring is more difficult. Behavioural activation is similarly well suited and simple to deliver."],
    ["What is cognitive stimulation therapy?","A structured group intervention for people with mild to moderate dementia, with NICE support and evidence for improvement in cognition and quality of life. It is one of the few non-pharmacological dementia interventions with a solid evidence base."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Common themes are loss, illness, dependency, fear of being a burden, and unresolved life material.",
    "Cohort effects mean psychological framing may be unfamiliar rather than unacceptable.",
    "Life review therapy, from Butler, has evidence in late-life depression.",
    "Reminiscence is less structured than life review and is used widely in dementia care.",
    "Group work addresses isolation, which is often a maintaining factor rather than only a consequence."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","gabbard","nice"] };

TEACH["8.9"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Old age brings a series of losses close together: a partner, friends, health, the house, the driving licence, the role. Grief is the normal response and it is not depression, but the two are hard to separate when the losses do not stop.",
  teach:[
    "Bereavement is the commonest major life event in later life, and multiple bereavements accumulate, so grief for one death occurs against the background of several.",
    "Normal grief in older people follows the same course as at any age: yearning, sadness, preoccupation, and often transient hallucinatory experiences of the deceased, which are normal.",
    "The distinction from depression rests on the same features as at any age: pervasive worthlessness, global guilt not related to the death, marked psychomotor retardation, and persistent suicidal ideation point to depression.",
    "Widowhood carries a substantially raised mortality in the months after the death, particularly for men, and the excess is largely cardiovascular and largely in the first six months.",
    "The widowhood effect is one of the more robust findings in social epidemiology, and it means the first months after a spouse's death are a period of genuine medical as well as psychiatric risk.",
    "Risk factors for complicated grief in later life: sudden or traumatic death, a highly dependent relationship, caring for the person before death, absence of other social support, and pre-existing depression.",
    "Anticipatory grief in carers of people with dementia is a distinct phenomenon. The person is lost progressively while still alive, and carers describe grieving before the death and sometimes relief after it, which produces guilt.",
    "Ambiguous loss, from Boss, names the situation in dementia where the person is physically present but psychologically absent. It has no ritual and no social recognition, which is part of its difficulty.",
    "Prolonged grief disorder in ICD-11 requires persistent longing or preoccupation with intense emotional pain beyond six months and exceeding cultural norms. DSM-5-TR uses twelve months.",
    "Adjustment disorder in later life follows the same principles but the stressors differ: diagnosis of illness, loss of driving licence, entry to residential care, and loss of independence.",
    "Relocation to residential care is a specific and under-recognised stressor, and the transition period carries raised mortality and raised risk of depression and delirium.",
    "Loss of a driving licence is frequently experienced as the end of independence rather than as an inconvenience, and its psychological impact is routinely underestimated by clinicians.",
    "Treatment: routine bereavement counselling for everyone is unhelpful and may worsen outcome, as at any age. Targeted intervention for those meeting criteria for prolonged grief disorder works, with grief-focused CBT having the best evidence.",
    "Antidepressants treat comorbid depression during bereavement but do not treat grief, and should not be withheld simply because a bereavement has occurred.",
    "Social intervention is frequently the effective treatment: re-establishing contact, practical help with the tasks the deceased performed, and structure to replace the routine that has been lost."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the widowhood effect?","A substantial rise in mortality in the months following the death of a spouse, greatest in men and concentrated in the first six months, largely cardiovascular in cause. It is one of the more robust findings in social epidemiology."],
    ["What is ambiguous loss?","Boss's concept describing the situation in dementia where the person is physically present but psychologically absent. There is no ritual, no formal recognition and no defined point of loss, which is part of what makes it difficult for carers."],
    ["Distinguish grief from depression in later life.","Pervasive worthlessness, global guilt unrelated to the death, marked psychomotor retardation and persistent suicidal ideation indicate depression. Yearning, sadness, preoccupation with the deceased and transient hallucinatory experiences of the deceased are features of normal grief."],
    ["Which transitions in later life are specific under-recognised stressors?","Relocation to residential care, which carries raised mortality and raised risk of depression and delirium during the transition; and loss of a driving licence, which is frequently experienced as the end of independence rather than an inconvenience."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Multiple bereavements accumulate, so grief occurs against a background of previous losses.",
    "Anticipatory grief in dementia carers may be followed by relief and consequent guilt.",
    "ICD-11 prolonged grief disorder requires six months; DSM-5-TR requires twelve.",
    "Routine bereavement counselling for all is unhelpful and may worsen outcome.",
    "Antidepressants should not be withheld simply because a bereavement has occurred."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","icd11","semliaison"] };

TEACH["8.10"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Sleep changes with age in normal ways that get treated as illness, and it also breaks in serious ways that get treated as normal ageing. Telling the two apart is most of the work.",
  teach:[
    "Normal age-related changes: reduced total sleep time, reduced slow wave sleep which declines markedly from midlife, increased sleep fragmentation with more awakenings, reduced sleep efficiency, and phase advance with earlier bedtime and earlier waking.",
    "Slow wave sleep declines most, and by late life may be almost absent in some people, which is a normal finding rather than pathology.",
    "REM sleep proportion is relatively preserved, though REM occurs earlier in the night.",
    "Phase advance means older people become sleepy earlier and wake earlier. Going to bed at eight and waking at four is a circadian phenomenon, not insomnia, and is treated with timed light exposure rather than hypnotics.",
    "Insomnia disorder affects a substantial proportion of older people, with prevalence higher than in younger adults, and is very frequently secondary rather than primary.",
    "Causes of secondary insomnia in later life: pain, nocturia, cardiac and respiratory disease, depression and anxiety, dementia, medication, caffeine and alcohol, daytime napping, and reduced daytime activity and light exposure.",
    "Nocturia is one of the commonest and least explored causes, and treating prostatic symptoms or reviewing diuretic timing can resolve what was presented as insomnia.",
    "Obstructive sleep apnoea prevalence rises with age and is heavily under-diagnosed. It causes daytime sleepiness, cognitive impairment, depression and treatment-resistant hypertension, and CPAP treatment improves all of them.",
    "Sleep apnoea is a specific and treatable contributor to apparent cognitive impairment, and should be considered in anyone with daytime somnolence and cognitive complaint.",
    "Restless legs syndrome affects a substantial proportion of older people, is worsened by antidepressants and antipsychotics, and is associated with iron deficiency, so ferritin should be checked.",
    "REM sleep behaviour disorder: loss of REM atonia with dream enactment, sometimes violent. It strongly predicts later alpha-synucleinopathy, particularly dementia with Lewy bodies and Parkinson's disease, with conversion in the majority over sufficient follow-up.",
    "That prediction is clinically important: REM sleep behaviour disorder is often the earliest sign of a neurodegenerative process, sometimes preceding it by a decade or more.",
    "Sundowning in dementia is increased confusion and agitation in the late afternoon and evening, related to circadian disruption, fatigue, reduced light and understimulation.",
    "Circadian rhythm disruption in dementia is substantial, with degeneration of the suprachiasmatic nucleus, and light therapy has modest supporting evidence.",
    "Treatment: address the cause first. CBT for insomnia is first-line and works in older people as well as in younger ones, with sleep restriction being the active component.",
    "Hypnotics in older people carry disproportionate risk: falls, hip fracture, cognitive impairment, delirium, road traffic accidents and dependence. Benzodiazepines and Z-drugs appear on the Beers and STOPP lists of drugs to avoid in this group.",
    "If a hypnotic is used, use the lowest dose for the shortest possible period with an explicit stop date, and never start one for a person newly admitted to hospital unless there is a clear indication.",
    "Melatonin is licensed for short-term use in adults over 55 and has a better safety profile, with modest efficacy.",
    "Deprescribing long-term hypnotics is a legitimate and achievable goal, and gradual withdrawal with CBT support is more effective than abrupt cessation."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Describe the normal age-related changes in sleep architecture.","Reduced total sleep time, markedly reduced slow wave sleep, increased fragmentation with more awakenings, reduced sleep efficiency, and circadian phase advance producing earlier sleep onset and earlier waking. REM proportion is relatively preserved though it occurs earlier in the night."],
    ["Why does REM sleep behaviour disorder matter in older people?","Because loss of REM atonia with dream enactment strongly predicts later alpha-synucleinopathy, particularly dementia with Lewy bodies and Parkinson's disease, with conversion in the majority over sufficient follow-up. It is often the earliest detectable sign, sometimes preceding the neurodegenerative disease by a decade or more."],
    ["Why should hypnotics be avoided in older people?","Because they carry disproportionate risk of falls, hip fracture, cognitive impairment, delirium, road traffic accidents and dependence. Benzodiazepines and Z-drugs appear on both the Beers and STOPP lists of drugs to avoid in this age group."],
    ["What is sundowning and what causes it?","Increased confusion, agitation and restlessness in the late afternoon and evening in people with dementia, related to circadian disruption from degeneration of the suprachiasmatic nucleus, accumulated fatigue, reduced light levels and understimulation."],
    ["Which treatable physical causes of insomnia are most often missed in older people?","Nocturia, which may resolve with treatment of prostatic symptoms or review of diuretic timing; obstructive sleep apnoea, which is heavily under-diagnosed and contributes to apparent cognitive impairment; and restless legs syndrome, which is associated with iron deficiency and worsened by antidepressants and antipsychotics."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Phase advance is circadian, not insomnia, and is treated with timed light exposure.",
    "Slow wave sleep may be nearly absent in late life as a normal finding.",
    "Check ferritin in restless legs syndrome.",
    "CBT for insomnia works as well in older people as in younger, with sleep restriction the active component.",
    "Melatonin is licensed for short-term use in adults over 55.",
    "Deprescribing long-term hypnotics with gradual withdrawal and CBT support is achievable."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","maudsley","nice"] };

TEACH["8.11"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Older people continue to have sexual lives, and services behave as though they do not. That silence causes real harm, from untreated dysfunction to unrecognised abuse in care homes.",
  teach:[
    "Sexual activity continues into late life for a substantial proportion of people, and the decline with age is far less steep than clinicians and the public assume.",
    "The English Longitudinal Study of Ageing and comparable surveys show that a majority of people in their sixties and seventies remain sexually active, and that interest persists even where activity declines.",
    "Partner availability, rather than desire or capacity, is the principal determinant of activity in later life, particularly for women, who are more likely to be widowed.",
    "Normal age-related changes: in men, slower erection, reduced rigidity, longer refractory period, and reduced ejaculatory volume. In women, reduced lubrication, vaginal atrophy and dyspareunia after the menopause.",
    "These changes are normal and are not dysfunction. They become a problem when they are misinterpreted, and simple explanation is frequently the whole treatment.",
    "Erectile dysfunction becomes common with age, and its causes are predominantly vascular, neurological, endocrine and iatrogenic rather than psychological in this age group.",
    "Erectile dysfunction is an independent marker of cardiovascular disease and should prompt cardiovascular assessment, not only a prescription.",
    "Drug causes are very common in this population: antihypertensives particularly beta-blockers and thiazides, SSRIs, antipsychotics, and anti-androgens for prostate cancer.",
    "Physical illness and disability affect sexual function through pain, fatigue, breathlessness, immobility, altered body image, and fear of precipitating a cardiac event.",
    "Post-myocardial infarction sexual anxiety is common in both patient and partner, and the actual cardiac risk of sexual activity is low for someone able to climb two flights of stairs without symptoms. Saying so explicitly is an effective intervention.",
    "Stoma formation, mastectomy, prostatectomy and amputation all affect body image and sexual function, and are rarely discussed in follow-up.",
    "Institutional settings raise the hardest questions. Care home residents have rights to private life under Article 8, and blanket prohibition of sexual expression is not lawful or defensible.",
    "Capacity to consent to sexual relations is decision-specific. The test concerns understanding of the sexual nature of the act, the reasonably foreseeable consequences, and the fact that it can be refused.",
    "The capacity threshold for sexual relations is deliberately set low, and the courts have held it to be act-specific rather than person-specific, which means it is not assessed in relation to a particular partner.",
    "Where one party has dementia, the situation requires careful individual assessment balancing protection from abuse against the right to a private life, ideally with a documented policy and multidisciplinary discussion rather than an ad hoc decision.",
    "Sexual disinhibition in dementia is distressing for families and staff. Assess for pain, delirium, unmet need, misidentification, and medication before treating the behaviour, and use non-pharmacological approaches first.",
    "Sexual abuse of older people occurs and is heavily under-detected, particularly in institutional settings and in people with dementia who cannot report it.",
    "Ask about sexual function. Older people rarely raise it, expect not to be asked, and frequently attribute treatable problems to age."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What most determines sexual activity in later life?","Partner availability rather than desire or physical capacity, particularly for women, who are more likely to be widowed. Interest frequently persists even where activity declines."],
    ["What is the test of capacity to consent to sexual relations?","Understanding the sexual nature of the act, its reasonably foreseeable consequences including pregnancy and sexually transmitted infection, and that it can be refused. The threshold is deliberately low, and the courts have held it to be act-specific rather than assessed in relation to a particular partner."],
    ["What advice should be given about sexual activity after myocardial infarction?","That the cardiac risk of sexual activity is low for a person able to climb two flights of stairs without symptoms. Anxiety in both patient and partner is common, and explicit reassurance is an effective intervention that is rarely given."],
    ["How should sexual disinhibition in dementia be approached?","By assessing first for pain, delirium, unmet need, misidentification of a person, constipation, and medication effects, and using non-pharmacological approaches before considering medication. It should be understood as behaviour with a cause rather than as a symptom to be suppressed."],
    ["What are the normal age-related sexual changes?","In men: slower erection, reduced rigidity, longer refractory period and reduced ejaculatory volume. In women: reduced lubrication, vaginal atrophy and dyspareunia after the menopause. These are normal changes rather than dysfunction, and explanation alone is frequently sufficient."]
  ],
  note:[
    "A majority of people in their sixties and seventies remain sexually active.",
    "Erectile dysfunction in older men is predominantly organic and is a marker of cardiovascular disease.",
    "Beta-blockers, thiazides, SSRIs, antipsychotics and anti-androgens are common drug causes.",
    "Care home residents have Article 8 rights; blanket prohibition of sexual expression is not defensible.",
    "Sexual abuse of older people is heavily under-detected, particularly in institutional settings."
  ],
  refs:["oxoldage","eastman","companion"] };
